D.2 Strong log-concavity and covariance bounds We repeatedly use the following standard consequences of Bakry–Émery curvature. If a probability measure \nu(\mathrm{d}z)\propto e^{-W(z)}\,\mathrm{d}z satisfies \nabla^{2}W\succeq mI , then, for smooth f , \Var_{\nu}(f)\leq\frac{1}{m}\,\mathbb{E}_{\nu}\|\nabla f\|^{2}\,, (D.6) and \nu satisfies the log-Sobolev inequality with constant 1/m . In particular, every L -Lipschitz f obeys Gaussian concentration \mathbb{P}\{f-\mathbb{E}f\geq t\}\leq\exp\Bigl(-\frac{mt^{2}}{2L^{2}}\Bigr)\,. (D.7) These statements follow from the Bakry–Émery criterion; see, for example, [ 3 , Chapters 4 and 5] . Lemma D.3 (Two-sided covariance bound) . Let \nu(\mathrm{d}z)\propto e^{-W(z)}\,\mathrm{d}z with mI\preceq\nabla^{2}W(z)\preceq LI\,. (D.8) Then L^{-1}I\preceq\mathrm{Cov}_{\nu}(Z)\preceq m^{-1}I\,. (D.9) Proof. The upper bound is the Brascamp–Lieb inequality [ 3 , Chapter 4] applied to linear functions. The lower bound follows from the Cramér–Rao inequality for the location family generated by \nu : \displaystyle\mathrm{Cov}_{\nu}(Z) \displaystyle\succeq\bigl(\mathbb{E}_{\nu}\nabla^{2}W(Z)\bigr)^{-1}\succeq L^{-1}I\,.\qed Hessian of the augmented marginal. Let \bar{x}_{y}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y=y] and \Sigma_{y}\coloneqq\eta^{-1}\,\mathrm{Cov}(X\mid Y=y) , as in the normalized coordinates of Appendix C . Differentiating the log-density of \pi_{\eta}^{Y} gives \nabla U_{\eta}(y)=\eta^{-1}(y-\bar{x}_{y})\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\nabla^{2}U_{\eta}(y)=\eta^{-1}(I-\Sigma_{y})\,. (D.10) The conditional potential in x has Hessian between (\alpha+\eta^{-1})I and (\beta+\eta^{-1})I . Applying Lemma D.3 in ( D.10 ) gives ( 2.13 ).